in this episode of cloud performance  atlas I helped some leaping lizards  track down why their bandwidth was off  in the weeds will we catch what's  bugging them stay tuned to find out  I got a nice call from the gecko  protocol team a b2b company offering a  custom lightweight networking protocol  built for gaming and other real-time  graphic systems their team was seeing  lower than expected throughput for their  back-end machines which are responsible  for transferring and transcoding large  video and graphics files their  architecture worked a little something  like this a client will push about 2  terabytes of data to cloud storage at  the same time it kicks off work request  to a front-end which leverages a TCP  load balancer to spin up a bunch of  compute engine instances in a single  region to feed on that data talk between  each other and get the work done  most of this was working as expected  however the gecko protocol group was  concerned about VM to VM throughput  where they were only seeing about 1.5  gigabit per second max connections I  duplicated their setup on my side which  involves a bunch of one CPU machines in  a single region and ran iperf on them  the result was about 1.9 five gigabits  per second which was much higher than  what gecko protocol was seeing now I  knew from experience that for high  bandwidth systems they were obviously  using too small machine for those  efforts since networking throughput is  related to core count bumping it up to  about 16 CPU machine in the US central  one region I was able to get about  fifteen point nine gigabits per second  obviously much better however when the  gecko protocol team switched over to  this configuration they didn't see the  same result they only ended up at about  one point six gigabits per second after  about twenty minutes of a video chat  trying to get our configurations  identical the solution smacked us right  in the face  the problem was that their team was  testing the connection to the external  IP of the server while I was testing on  the internal IP see if two machines are  communicating using their internal IPs  then they can send traffic on Google's  private Internet which is super fast  using the public IP however forces the  data packet to get pushed to the public  Internet before coming back to the  machine aka not the fastest when we  switched over their configuration to use  the internal IPS with the larger machine  set up the bandwidth went through the  roof fifteen point nine gigabits per  second for gecko protocol this small  debugging session resulted in their  video and graphics data transfer rate  improving  by about 14x for more great cloud  content subscribe to the Google cloud  YouTube channel and stay up to date with  other cloud performance Atlas video  content because when it comes to  performance every millisecond counts
 

